    id: 190,
    slug: "dhs-hsin-homeland-security-information-network-breach-july-2026",
    title: "DHS Confirms Hackers Breached Its HSIN Information-Sharing Network",
    summary: "The Homeland Security Information Network and an associated SharePoint system were compromised between late May and early June; DHS hasn't named an actor or confirmed data theft.",
    body: [
      "The Department of Homeland Security is investigating a breach of the Homeland Security Information Network, the platform federal, state, local, and private-sector partners use to share sensitive threat information.",
      "According to DHS, the intrusion happened somewhere between late May and early June, and hit both HSIN servers and an associated SharePoint system. The department confirmed the compromise this week but has not attributed it to any actor, and says it's still unclear whether documents were actually stolen. In a statement, DHS said it isolated the affected systems and launched a forensic investigation, that there's no indication classified networks were touched, and that the platform remains operational for its partners.",
      "HSIN isn't classified, but it's not nothing. It's where partners coordinate on physical and cyber threats, and a lot of that traffic is exactly the kind of operational detail an adversary would want. The timing draws extra scrutiny because it lands in the middle of World Cup 2026 security planning, a period when that coordination network is busier than usual.",
      "The SharePoint detail is worth sitting with. On-prem SharePoint has been one of the most reliably exploited pieces of enterprise software this year, with CISA adding a fresh SharePoint remote-code-execution bug to its exploited-vulnerabilities catalog just last week. DHS hasn't said whether a SharePoint flaw was the way in, but the pairing of HSIN servers and an associated SharePoint system will make every incident responder assume it until told otherwise.",
      "The open questions are the ones DHS hasn't answered: what was accessed, for how long, and by whom. Until there's a scoping statement, any partner whose data touched HSIN in that late-May-to-early-June window should treat it as potentially exposed.",
    ],
