      "TeraWulf, a Nasdaq-listed outfit that made its name mining crypto, announced the deal on July 6. Anthropic will occupy a data center TeraWulf is building on the site of a former aluminum smelter in Hawesville, Kentucky, which will run at 401 megawatts once it's fully online. Initial capacity is slated for the second half of 2027, with the full build following the year after.",
      "The math explains why miners keep making this jump. TeraWulf plans to sink $3 to $4 billion into the site, less than a fifth of the lease's total value, against roughly $19 billion in contracted revenue over the term. For a company with a market value around $12 billion, a single tenant just committed to paying more than the whole company is worth. Shares rose about 5%.",
      "The aluminum-smelter detail isn't incidental. Those sites exist because someone already ran high-voltage power to them decades ago, and cheap, available power is the one thing AI buildouts can't conjure on demand. Old heavy industry is being recycled into compute because the grid connection is the scarce asset, not the land or the concrete.",
      "The broader pattern is a capital reshuffle hiding inside a real estate deal. Crypto miners spent years building power-hungry sites on a bet about Bitcoin; now the surer bet is renting those sites to AI labs on 20-year terms. Anthropic gets guaranteed capacity without owning the buildings. TeraWulf gets a revenue floor that dwarfs its mining business. Whether 401 megawatts still counts as a lot of compute in 2028 is the part nobody's underwriting.",
